# QUALITY CONTROL — DON'T LET ANYONE FEEL STUPID.
#
# The job is not "find bugs". It is: do not let a user end up in a dumb
# situation that makes them feel dumb.
#
# That is a different bar and it changes what counts. A crash is HONEST — the
# user knows it was not them. The enemy is the quiet divergence: they wrote
# `as:` and it was accepted and did nothing; a query and a read model drifted
# apart with neither saying so; a value they supplied was dropped on the way
# in. Nothing errored. The chapter promised something, the runtime did
# otherwise, and the only person who finds out concludes they do not
# understand their own domain.
#
# ALL THREE ARE COMPARISONS, which is why a crash announces itself and a
# broken promise does not: declaration against behaviour, one answer against
# another, what was sent against what was stored. You only see it holding two
# things side by side. So the unit of work here is not "a bug" — it is a
# CHECK: a dispatch made with an expectation written first, and an answer
# compared against it afterwards.
#
# THE UNIT OF ATTENTION IS A CHAPTER. Fixing what Banking exposed usually
# fixes Pizzas too, because the fault is nearly always in the runtime rather
# than in the chapter that revealed it. So the practice rotates: least
# recently swept first, one agent at a time, and a claim that goes stale is
# taken by whoever is next.
#
# ONE ABSOLUTE RULE, AND EVERYTHING ELSE BENDS. A bug is logged with the
# failing test that proves it, or it is not logged. That is a required
# ARGUMENT, not a gate — an argument can be satisfied or refused and never
# waived — and it is absolute because without it there is no evidence. Every
# other rule here is a gate: it refuses, it can be waived with a reason, and
# the waiver is counted so "how often do we go round ourselves" is a number
# rather than a feeling.

# THE PRACTICE'S OWN DIALS — HARDCODED HERE, NOT IN PROSE AN LLM READS.
#
# `.claude/skills/hecks_qa/SKILL.md` used to say "cap: 3 draft PRs per day"
# in a sentence, which meant changing it meant editing instructions written
# for an agent to follow rather than editing data — nobody could grep for
# the number, and nothing enforced that the sentence and the practice ever
# agreed. Here, it is a fact about the file, the same way `StaleAfter`'s 900
# or `BugOrder`'s 1_000 already are: a human changes it by editing this file
# and reviewing the diff, not by asking a running agent to remember
# something different.
#
# NO AGGREGATE, ON PURPOSE. A live, dispatchable "Policy" record was the
# first shape tried here and taken back — these two numbers are a human's
# call, not a fact the loop should ever mutate about itself mid-run, and a
# bare Ruby constant is the plainest way to write "this is fixed until a
# person changes it" in a language that also has commands for the things
# that are NOT fixed. (Also: `Policy` collides with this very file's own
# reaction-policy keyword a few hundred lines down — see the `BugTitle`
# comment on prefix collisions for why that is not hypothetical here.)
#
# GUARDED AGAINST RELOAD, NOT JUST DEFINED. This file is `load`ed once per
# `Hecks.boot` — and `spec/quality_control_spec.rb` boots a fresh runtime
# per example, dozens of times in one process. A bare `CONST = value` at
# top level is reassigned every single time that happens, and Ruby warns
# on every one of those forty-some reassignments. `defined?` makes the
# second-and-later load a no-op instead of a warning generator.
unless defined?(QualityControlDials)
  module QualityControlDials
    # HOW LONG `hecks_qa`'s OWN LOOP WAITS AFTER A SWEEP CONCLUDES BEFORE
    # STARTING THE NEXT ONE. Zero means immediately: the loop restarts the
    # moment the previous sweep's report comes back, rather than waiting
    # out a fixed interval. This number is read as a CEILING, not a pace —
    # the longest the orchestrator will EVER go without hearing back
    # before it assumes a subagent stalled and starts again anyway.
    CADENCE_SECONDS = 0

    # HOW MANY draft PRs `hecks_qa` may open in one calendar day. Zero
    # means uncapped. A positive number here is a fence for when nobody is
    # watching the loop closely; a human who IS watching it can say so, in
    # this file, rather than the loop inventing a limit nobody chose.
    PR_CAP_PER_DAY = 0

    # WHAT FRACTION OF GENERATED COMMAND STEPS `bin/qa_sweep` MUTATES
    # ADVERSARIALLY (0.0..1.0) — `SequenceGenerator`'s `adversarial:`
    # option, `lib/hecks/fuzzing/sequence_generator/adversary.rb`: the
    # argument shapes BUG#7–#16 were actually found through, injected on
    # every domain in the rotation rather than only the one that happened
    # to declare the colliding name. Zero turns the layer off (a sweep
    # then generates exactly what it did before the layer existed).
    # Higher finds refusal-ordering splits sooner but reaches deep state
    # less often — a mutated step is usually refused, and a refused step
    # creates nothing later steps can act on. `bin/qa_sweep --adversarial
    # N` overrides this for one run.
    ADVERSARIAL_FRACTION = 0.3

    # WHETHER `bin/qa_sweep` ALSO RUNS THE SELF-CONSISTENCY PASS — cold
    # rehydration, replay idempotency, value-object JSON round trip
    # (`Hecks::Fuzzing::SelfConsistency`) — beside its own, older
    # differential (Ruby vs compiled Rust) comparison, on the SAME
    # generated sequence (adversarially mutated or not — the two layers
    # compose for free: this pass reads whatever `history` the dispatch
    # of THAT sequence produced, adversarial or plain, with no idea which
    # it was). ON by default: this is a genuinely different comparison
    # axis (one engine against ITSELF, not one engine against the other),
    # not an experimental extra, so the practice's own default posture is
    # to run it every sweep. `--self-consistency false` (or `true`)
    # overrides this for one run the same way `--adversarial` overrides
    # `ADVERSARIAL_FRACTION`.
    SELF_CONSISTENCY_CHECKS = true

    # THE THREE DIALS BELOW ARE DIFFERENT FROM `CADENCE_SECONDS`/
    # `PR_CAP_PER_DAY` ABOVE IN ONE WAY WORTH FLAGGING (the same way
    # `ADVERSARIAL_FRACTION` just above already is): those two are read
    # by an AGENT following `.claude/skills/hecks_qa/SKILL.md`'s own
    # prose — nothing in this repository's Ruby ever names them. These
    # three, like `ADVERSARIAL_FRACTION`, are read in CODE —
    # `Hecks::Fuzzing::RotationPriority`, every time `bin/qa_sweep` picks
    # a single target with no name given — because the weighting formula
    # this task exists to add has to be a pure function of ledger state,
    # not a "please remember to weight it" instruction, so the numbers
    # it runs on have to be data a program reads too, not only a human.

    # HOW MANY SECONDS OF STALENESS ONE POINT OF A TARGET'S OWN DECAYED
    # YIELD SCORE IS WORTH. `RotationPriority.pick` adds
    # `yield_score.value * YIELD_WEIGHT_SECONDS` straight onto a target's
    # own staleness in seconds before comparing candidates, so this reads
    # literally as "one more bug-bearing release is worth this many
    # seconds of head start over a target that has not been swept in a
    # while." Zero would make yield invisible and put the pick straight
    # back to pure least-recently-swept — unlike the two dials above,
    # that is not a real "uncapped" escape hatch here, just a small
    # weight, so pick a number believing the practice actually wants
    # yield to matter.
    YIELD_WEIGHT_SECONDS = 1_800

    # WHAT SURVIVES ONE RELEASE, as a percentage of the OLD score. A
    # target's yield score is not a running total of every bug it has
    # ever produced — a chapter that found three things two months ago
    # and has been clean for forty sweeps since should not still
    # out-rank one nobody has looked at all week, which a running total
    # would do forever. 50 means roughly the last two or three releases'
    # worth of findings dominate the number, which is what "recent"
    # yield is supposed to mean. The decay itself happens in the CALLER,
    # never inside `Target.Release` — see that command's own comment for
    # why the mutation language cannot express "decay, then add" against
    # one field in one command.
    YIELD_DECAY_PERCENT = 50

    # THE FLOOR. Weighting toward yield is supposed to sharpen the
    # rotation, not let an exhausted target starve just because
    # something else keeps finding bugs — a target can go quiet for a
    # long stretch and then find something again, the way roster did
    # across a whole session's worth of otherwise-clean sweeps
    # elsewhere. Past this many seconds since it was last swept, a
    # waiting target wins `RotationPriority.pick` outright, yield score
    # or not — see that module's own comment for the exact mechanics.
    # Six hours is long enough that it never fires in one ordinary
    # sweeping session and short enough that nothing sits untouched for
    # days.
    ROTATION_STALE_FLOOR_SECONDS = 21_600

    # THE PERSISTENCE-PARITY AXIS'S OWN SPEED FENCE — `bin/qa_sweep
    # --persistence-parity` (lib/hecks/fuzzing/persistence_parity.rb) pays
    # for a real `PG.connect` and real SQL per dispatch, against a real
    # disposable `PostgresEra` database, where every OTHER check this
    # practice runs (Memory-vs-Rust, Memory-only properties) pays for
    # neither. Clamped here, not left to whatever `--seeds` a human or an
    # agent happens to type — the same reason `CADENCE_SECONDS`/
    # `PR_CAP_PER_DAY` live as data instead of prose: a human changes the
    # number by editing this file and reviewing the diff, not by asking a
    # running agent to remember a different one. `bin/qa_sweep` reads this
    # as a CEILING on `--seeds`, clamping down (never up) and printing a
    # note when it does — a smaller `--seeds` for an ordinary quick check
    # is still honoured exactly as given.
    PERSISTENCE_PARITY_SEED_CAP = 5
  end
end

Hecks.bluebook "QualityControl" do
  vision "A user should never be made to feel stupid by something that took what they said and quietly did otherwise. Every check writes down what it expected before it looks, every bug carries the test that proves it, and nothing reaches the outside world that nobody first tried to fix."

  core

  # ── whose turn it is ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

  # THE ROTATION, AND THE LOCK.
  #
  # The only aggregate here whose whole reason is coordination: more than one
  # QA agent runs at a time, and two of them starting on Banking is wasted
  # work and duplicate bugs. `Claim` is a lifecycle edge, so the race resolves
  # itself — one transition wins and the other agent is refused and takes the
  # next chapter. Nothing needed a lock; the machine already is one.
  aggregate "Target" do
    description "A chapter that can be pressure-tested, and whose turn it is — the rotation, and the claim that stops two agents doing the same work twice."

    identified_by :reference

    attribute :reference,  TargetReference
    attribute :path,       TargetPath
    attribute :held_by,    Engineer
    # WHEN THE CLAIM WAS TAKEN, as a number. Not a timestamp type: the
    # predicate sublanguage compares numbers and nothing else, and the
    # staleness rule below has to be EXPRESSIBLE, not merely true.
    attribute :claimed_at, Instant
    attribute :window,     StaleAfter
    attribute :last_swept, SweptIn
    # A DECAYED COUNT OF HOW OFTEN THIS CHAPTER HAS RECENTLY BEEN WORTH
    # SWEEPING — see `YieldScore`'s own comment for what it holds and
    # `QualityControlDials::YIELD_WEIGHT_SECONDS`/`RotationPriority` for
    # what it is FOR. Zero, same as `SweptIn`, means "nothing recent to
    # weight toward" — a fresh target and a long-clean one both start
    # here, which is exactly right: neither has EARNED priority yet.
    attribute :yield_score, YieldScore
    # HOW MANY CONSECUTIVE `Release`s IN A ROW CAME BACK CLEAN — not a
    # count of sweeps ever run, a count of sweeps run SINCE THE LAST TIME
    # ANYTHING SURPRISED THIS TARGET. `bin/qa_sweep` reads it right after
    # claiming, to decide how hard to fuzz THIS pass (see that file's own
    # header on the widening formula): a target that has held clean fifty
    # times running is under-tested by a fixed shallow sweep the same way
    # a target just added to the rotation is — the streak is what tells
    # the runner which of those two situations it is actually in.
    #
    # ZERO MEANS "NO STREAK YET" — a freshly `Identify`d target (never
    # swept), and a target whose last sweep ever surprised, read
    # identically here, on purpose: both deserve the narrowest, cheapest
    # pass until they have proven themselves clean again. Same shape
    # `SweptIn`'s own "zero sorts first for free" comment already gives.
    attribute :clean_streak, CleanStreak
    # WHAT THIS CHAPTER CAN BE COMPARED AGAINST — comma-joined mode names
    # (`"rust,postgres_era"`), recorded by whoever releases it from what
    # that runner actually inferred from the chapter's own files (a
    # `rust/Cargo.toml` feature, a `persisted_by("PostgresEra")` binding).
    # STORED, NOT DECIDED: inference at sweep time stays the decider of
    # which modes run; this field only makes that decision LEDGER-VISIBLE
    # (`EligibleFor`, below), so "why did directory never get a parity
    # pass" is a query rather than an archaeology. A stored list that
    # lags yesterday's Cargo feature is itself the quiet divergence this
    # practice hunts — see `Capabilities`'s own comment on the shape.
    attribute :capabilities, Capabilities
    attribute :reason,     TargetReason

    value_object "TargetReference" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a target is referenced") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # WHERE IT ACTUALLY IS. Required, because "Banking" is a name two people
    # can disagree about and a path is a thing you can open.
    value_object "TargetPath" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a target says where it is") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "Engineer" do
      attribute :value, String, default: "nobody"
    end

    value_object "Instant" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("an instant is not before the epoch") { !value.negative? }
    end

    # WHEN IT WAS LAST SWEPT, AS A NUMBER — and the first version of this was
    # the string "never", which does not sort. `order_by :last_swept` put
    # "SW-9" before "never" because S precedes n in ASCII, so the rotation
    # offered the recently-swept chapter first and the never-swept one last:
    # exactly backwards, silently, which is the failure this whole chapter
    # exists to hunt. Caught by its own spec.
    #
    # Zero means never, and zero sorts first for free.
    value_object "SweptIn" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("a sweep time is not before the epoch") { !value.negative? }
    end

    # A COUNT, NOT A CLOCK — unlike every other Integer VO in this
    # aggregate (`Instant`, `SweptIn`), nothing here compares this against
    # `now`. `default: 0` is what makes this field ADDITIVE to a live
    # ledger: an existing `Target` row written before this field existed
    # hydrates with `clean_streak.value == 0` rather than refusing to
    # boot — the exact same "zero means never" shape `SweptIn` already
    # established for a row from before ITS own field existed.
    value_object "CleanStreak" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("a streak is not negative") { !value.negative? }
    end

    value_object "TargetReason" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("shelving a target says why") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # COMMA-JOINED, ONE STRING — the `Bug.Tagged` shape (`contains`
    # against a bound argument) without a `list_of`: `EligibleFor` asks
    # "does this chapter carry mode X" and `contains` answers it as a
    # substring on a scalar, which is exactly what a comma-joined list of
    # short, distinct mode names needs and nothing more. `default: ""` is
    # what makes this ADDITIVE to a live ledger, the same `CleanStreak`
    # precedent: a `Target` row written before this field existed
    # hydrates as "nothing recorded yet", not as a refusal to boot.
    value_object "Capabilities" do
      attribute :value, String, default: ""
    end

    # ONE MODE NAME, as `EligibleFor`'s own argument — a query attribute,
    # never stored on the record, so it costs no era.
    value_object "Capability" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a mode is named") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # RECENCY-WEIGHTED, NOT LIFETIME. This is not "how many bugs this
    # chapter has ever produced" — that number only grows, and would let
    # one hot month outrank every other target forever. It is the
    # decayed count `Hecks::Fuzzing::RotationPriority.next_yield_score`
    # computes at each `Release`: mostly this period's own findings,
    # fading out what came before by `QualityControlDials::
    # YIELD_DECAY_PERCENT` every time. An Integer, like `CheckCount` and
    # `BugOrder` elsewhere in this chapter — a priority signal, not a
    # statistic anyone reports on its own.
    value_object "YieldScore" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("a yield score is not negative") { !value.negative? }
    end

    lifecycle :status, default: "waiting" do
      transition "Claim"   => "held",    from: ["waiting", "held"]
      transition "Release" => "waiting", from: "held"
      transition "Shelve"  => "shelved", from: ["waiting", "held"]
      transition "Restore" => "waiting", from: "shelved"
    end

    command "Identify" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Write down that a chapter exists which nobody has swept"

      attribute :reference, TargetReference
      attribute :path,      TargetPath

      emits "TargetIdentified"
    end

    # THE STALE CLAIM, IN THE ARITHMETIC THE LANGUAGE ACTUALLY HAS.
    #
    # `from: ["waiting", "held"]` looks wrong and is the point: a held target
    # CAN be claimed, but only once its claim has gone stale. The rule reads
    # "it is not held, or the claim is old enough", and it is spelled as an
    # addition because the sublanguage has `+` and no `-` — `claimed_at +
    # window <= now` says what `now - claimed_at >= window` would, in the
    # words available.
    #
    # `now` ARRIVES AS AN ARGUMENT, from the Clock port. A predicate cannot
    # ask the time — it reads attributes and nothing else — so the caller
    # supplies it, exactly as it supplies an id. The port is what makes that
    # testable instead of dependent on wall-clock luck.
    command "Claim" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Take the next chapter in the rotation, or one whose holder has gone quiet"

      # THE WINDOW IS NOT THE CLAIMER'S TO SET. It was an argument, which meant
      # any agent could take a live claim from any other by asking with a window
      # of one second — the guard read a number the CALLER supplied and dutifully
      # agreed. A staleness rule that the party it constrains gets to choose is
      # not a rule.
      #
      # So it lives on the record, where `given` reads it as state and no dispatch
      # can name it. Fifteen minutes is the practice's answer, written once as a
      # default rather than re-asserted by every claimer.
      reference_to Target
      attribute :held_by,    Engineer
      attribute :now,        Instant

      given("a live claim is not taken from the agent holding it") do
        status != "held" || claimed_at.value + window.value <= now.value
      end

      sets :held_by
      sets :claimed_at, to: :now

      emits "TargetClaimed"
    end

    value_object "StaleAfter" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 900
      invariant("a staleness window is positive") { value.positive? }
    end

    command "Release" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Hand a chapter back to the rotation, stamped with when, what this pass was worth, and how the clean streak moves"

      # THE ARGUMENT IS `now`, AND THE FIELD IS `last_swept`, which is what
      # `sets` is for. It was `last_swept` on both sides and that made it
      # the one timestamp a caller still had to type by hand — the door fills
      # `now` and knows nothing about a field called `last_swept`. Naming the
      # ARGUMENT for where it comes from and the FIELD for what it means costs
      # one line and leaves no hand-typed clock anywhere in the chapter.
      #
      # `next_streak` IS THE SAME SHAPE, ONE FIELD OVER — and it is the
      # CALLER'S to compute, not this record's, for the identical reason
      # `now` is: whether the sweep just concluded was clean is a fact
      # about `Sweep` (and, for any check ever marked `Surprised`, about
      # that check's own sticky `ever_surprised` — see `Sweep::Check::
      # Surprised`'s own comment — which survives even a later `Remake`
      # that resolves it), and `Target` cannot read another aggregate's
      # state to check that for itself. No policy could carry it either —
      # see this file's OWN closing comment on why the rotation gets no
      # automatic reaction at all: the payload a `SweepConcluded` event
      # carries has no way to reach back into `Sweep.checks` for this, and
      # a reaction that cannot carry what its target needs refuses at run
      # time while reading, in the log, as though it had worked. So this
      # is REQUIRED, not defaulted or left optional the way an absent fact
      # quietly resolves to nil elsewhere in this language — an omitted
      # streak would either silently blank a real counter or silently
      # guess at continuing it, and neither is a guess this record should
      # make about work it never watched happen.
      #
      # `bin/qa_sweep` already has to read THIS record's own `clean_streak`
      # before it ever claims, to size the very sweep it is about to run —
      # so computing `clean_streak.value + 1` (every check held, and none
      # of them ever surprised) or `0` (anything did) costs it nothing
      # extra, and keeps the arithmetic out of the one record that cannot
      # check it for itself.
      reference_to Target
      attribute :now,          Instant
      attribute :next_streak,  CleanStreak

      # REQUIRED, NOT DEFAULTED TO ZERO — the same call `Submitter` already
      # makes over `Engineer`'s "nobody" default (see that value object's own
      # comment): a caller that could silently release a target at yield
      # zero would quietly erase whatever it actually found, the exact
      # failure this whole chapter exists to hunt. The releasing caller
      # already has the target's OLD `yield_score` in hand (it just claimed
      # or is holding this very record) and knows how many things this pass
      # actually turned up, so it computes the next value itself —
      # `Hecks::Fuzzing::RotationPriority.next_yield_score` — and this
      # command only stores what it is told, the same division of labor
      # `now`/`last_swept` already have.
      #
      # WHY THE DECAY DOES NOT LIVE HERE, AS ITS OWN MUTATION. The
      # mutation language has both `multiply` (decay) and `increment` (add
      # this period's finds) — but a command's declared mutations are one
      # update set, and build refuses a field written twice
      # (`MutationApplier`'s own comment on `CommandBuilder#refuse_
      # duplicate_targets!`). `sets :yield_score, multiply: ...` and a
      # second `sets :yield_score, increment: ...` right after it is not two
      # steps, it is a build-time refusal. So the arithmetic happens exactly
      # where `now` already happens — in the caller — and only the finished
      # number crosses into the aggregate.
      attribute :yield_score, YieldScore

      sets :last_swept, to: :now
      sets :yield_score
      sets :clean_streak, to: :next_streak

      emits "TargetReleased"
    end

    command "Shelve" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Decide a chapter is not worth the practice's time, and say why"

      reference_to Target
      attribute :reason, TargetReason

      sets :reason

      emits "TargetShelved"
    end

    command "Restore" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Put a shelved chapter back in the rotation"

      reference_to Target
      emits "TargetRestored"
    end

    # STILL ORDERED BY `last_swept` ALONE, DELIBERATELY — `order_by`
    # sorts by exactly one stored field (`Ports::Query::Ordering`), and a
    # target's actual rotation PRIORITY wants two blended together: time
    # since last swept, AND recent yield (`yield_score`, now a field on
    # every row this returns). Nothing in the query sublanguage can
    # express that blend, and computing it here would mean this query's
    # answer changes with the wall clock even between two reads in the
    # same request — which `where`/`order_by` never do anywhere else in
    # this chapter. So this stays the raw view — useful on its own, "what
    # has gone longest untouched" — and `Hecks::Fuzzing::RotationPriority
    # .pick` is what actually weights it, against the rows this returns,
    # in Ruby, where `now` can be an argument instead of a lie. See that
    # module's own header for exactly what it changes: `bin/qa_sweep`
    # picking ONE target with no name given, and nothing about `bin/
    # qa_sweep --all`, which sweeps every row here regardless of order.
    query "Rotation" do
      description "Whose turn it is — waiting chapters, least recently swept first. The one reason this aggregate exists. Raw ordering only — see Hecks::Fuzzing::RotationPriority for the yield-weighted pick built on top of these same rows."
      where(status: "waiting")
      order_by :last_swept
    end

    query "Untouched" do
      description "Never swept at all — zero is the epoch and means nobody has looked. The gap no count of checks can show you, because an unswept chapter leaves no rows anywhere."
      where(status: "waiting")
      where(:"last_swept.value" => 0)
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "Held" do
      description "Claimed right now, and by whom. Read it before wondering why the rotation looks short — a claim older than the window is free for the taking."
      where(status: "held")
      order_by :claimed_at
    end

    query "Shelved" do
      description "Deliberately not swept, each with its reason. Re-read before anybody claims a coverage number."
      where(status: "shelved")
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "All" do
      description "Every chapter ever written down — the inventory a runner walking the repository checks its findings against."
      order_by :reference
    end
  end

  # ── one pass over one chapter ────────────────────────────────────────

  aggregate "Sweep" do
    description "One agent's pass over one chapter — every check it made, what each expected, and what actually came back."

    reference_to Target

    identified_by :reference

    attribute :reference, SweepReference
    attribute :engineer,  Engineer
    attribute :made,      CheckCount
    attribute :notes,     SweepNotes
    # THE WAIVER COUNT — the mark that makes going round a gate countable. A
    # counter rather than a flag because a sweep can bend more than one rule,
    # and rather than a list because a `where` cannot ask whether a list is
    # empty.
    attribute :waived,    WaiverCount
    # WHO WAIVED — the signature beside the count. `waived` says a gate
    # was gone round; this says by whom, and `WaivedBy`'s own invariants
    # are what make "never waive it yourself" a REFUSAL rather than a
    # sentence in SKILL.md an agent had to remember. Absent on every
    # sweep that never waived anything, and on every row written before
    # this field existed — no default, on purpose: a default signature
    # would be a forged one.
    attribute :waived_by, WaivedBy
    attribute :checks,    list_of(Check)

    value_object "SweepReference" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a sweep is referenced") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "Engineer" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a sweep says who ran it") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "CheckCount" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("a check count is not negative") { !value.negative? }
    end

    value_object "WaiverCount" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("a waiver count is not negative") { !value.negative? }
    end

    value_object "SweepNotes" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      # THE NUMBER IS IN THE SENTENCE, because the sentence IS the refusal.
      # "a sweep says what it learned" told a caller their note was wrong and
      # not one thing about what would make it right, which is the exact
      # failure this chapter opens by naming.
      invariant("a sweep says what it learned, in at least 40 characters") { value.to_s.size >= 40 }
    end

    value_object "WaiverReason" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a waived gate says why") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # THE UNWAIVABLE SHAPE, APPLIED TO WAIVING ITSELF. This chapter's own
    # header: an argument is satisfied or refused and never waived. "Never
    # waive it yourself" used to be a bullet in SKILL.md — a rule an agent
    # was asked to hold in mind — and now it is a required argument whose
    # invariants refuse the loop's own identity outright. The literal
    # `"qa_sweep"` here IS `QualityControlDials::AUTOMATED_ENGINEER`; the
    # invariant grammar reads literals, not constants, so the two are
    # pinned equal by `spec/quality_control_spec.rb` rather than by
    # reference. `"nobody"` is refused for the reason `Submitter` already
    # gives: an unsigned waiver is an anonymous one.
    value_object "WaivedBy" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a gate is waived by a person, never by the loop") { value != "qa_sweep" }
      invariant("a waiver is signed") { value != "nobody" }
    end

    # THE TARGET, RESTATED ON A SURPRISE so the EVENT carries it — the
    # `Bug.Fix` precedent (that command's own `reference` comment gives
    # the full reasoning): an emitted event carries exactly the arguments
    # it was dispatched with, never the record's own references, so
    # `CheckSurprised` said `sequence` and `observation` and nothing a
    # policy could address a `Target` by. `SuspendOnSurprise` (this file's
    # own foot) is the reaction that needs it.
    value_object "TargetRef" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "CheckSequence" do
      attribute :value, Integer
      invariant("a check sequence is positive") { value.positive? }
    end

    # WHAT WAS PUT TO THE SYSTEM — a verb, a script, a suite. Text, because
    # the battery derives its subjects from the chapter's own command surface
    # at run time, and a closed set here would refuse whatever the language
    # grew last week.
    # PLAIN TEXT, NO PATTERN — and that is the point. Every other string in
    # this chapter refuses whitespace, because a reference or a name that is
    # all spaces is a mistake somebody wants caught early. A test file is
    # newlines by construction, and tool output is whatever the tool said.
    value_object "Listing" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "Subject" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a check names what it put to the system") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # WHAT WAS SUPPOSED TO HAPPEN, WRITTEN BEFORE IT DID.
    #
    # The load-bearing rule of this aggregate, and the reason a check is a
    # record rather than a line in a log. A comparison needs two sides and one
    # of them has to exist first: an expectation filled in after the answer is
    # an expectation fitted to the answer, and a check that cannot be
    # surprised finds nothing.
    value_object "Expectation" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a check says what it expected") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "Observation" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # ONE COMPARISON.
    #
    # AN ENTITY, and the test is the one `docs/guides/entities.md` states: a
    # check has its own identity, its own verbs and its own lifecycle, and
    # never makes sense apart from the sweep that made it. Nobody looks up
    # check 7 without already knowing which sweep ran it.
    #
    # The ask that would argue for a head — "show me every surprising check
    # across every sweep" — is answered anyway: an entity query returns every
    # matching element across every parent, stamped with the parent it came
    # from. `Surprising` is that ask.
    entity "Check" do
      description "One dispatch made against a written-down expectation, and what actually came back."

      identified_by :sequence

      attribute :sequence,    CheckSequence
      attribute :subject,     Subject
      attribute :expectation, Expectation
      attribute :observation, Observation, optional: true
      # STICKY, ON PURPOSE — `outcome` (below) is the CURRENT verdict, and
      # `Remake` deliberately puts it back to "made" so a check that was
      # once surprising can be re-run against a system that has since
      # changed. That is exactly right for the OUTCOME and exactly wrong
      # for the QUESTION `Target::Release`'s own `next_streak` needs
      # answered: "did anything in this sweep EVER surprise", which a
      # `Remake`-then-`Held` cycle must never be able to erase. `default:
      # "no"` rather than a `Boolean` type: this chapter has no precedent
      # anywhere for a boolean-typed attribute, and `one_of` — already the
      # established closed-set idiom here (`SafeDepositBox.size`'s own
      # comment on the inline shorthand) — is one more construct nobody
      # reading this file has to learn.
      attribute :ever_surprised, one_of("no", "yes"), default: { value: "no" }

      # `surprising` IS NOT `failed`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE POINT. A check
      # that refused when it should have refused is `held`. A check that did
      # something other than what was written down is `surprising` — which
      # covers the crash AND the quiet divergence without prejudging which,
      # and the quiet one is the whole reason this chapter exists.
      lifecycle :outcome, default: "made" do
        transition "Held"      => "held",       from: "made"
        transition "Surprised" => "surprising", from: "made"
        transition "Unsettled" => "unsettled",  from: "made"
        transition "Remake"    => "made",       from: ["held", "surprising", "unsettled"]
      end

      command "Held" do
        role "QA engineer"
        goal "Record that the system did what the chapter promised"

        attribute :sequence,    CheckSequence
        attribute :observation, Observation

        sets :observation

        emits "CheckHeld"
      end

      command "Surprised" do
        role "QA engineer"
        goal "Record that the system did something other than what was written down"

        attribute :sequence,    CheckSequence
        attribute :observation, Observation

        sets :observation
        # `to: "yes"` — A LITERAL, NEVER `Remake`d BACK. `ever_surprised`'s
        # own comment is the full reasoning; this is the one place it is
        # ever set, and nothing else in this entity ever sets it again.
        sets :ever_surprised, to: "yes"

        emits "CheckSurprised"
      end

      command "Unsettled" do
        role "QA engineer"
        goal "Record a check that ran and settled nothing"

        attribute :sequence,    CheckSequence
        attribute :observation, Observation

        sets :observation

        emits "CheckUnsettled"
      end

      command "Remake" do
        role "QA engineer"
        goal "Put a settled check back, against a system that has since changed"

        attribute :sequence, CheckSequence

        emits "CheckRemade"
      end

      query "Surprising" do
        description "Every check the system surprised, across every sweep — the raw material a bug is written from, and the only list here that is supposed to be interesting."
        where(outcome: "surprising")
        order_by :sequence
      end

      query "Unsettled" do
        description "Made and never ruled on, plus the runs that settled nothing. A sweep concluded with rows here concluded early."
        where(outcome: { in: "made,unsettled" })
        order_by :sequence
      end

      query "ForSubject" do
        description "Everything ever put to one verb, across every sweep. Count the rows and that is its coverage."
        attribute :subject, Subject
        where(subject: :subject)
        order_by :sequence
      end
    end

    lifecycle :status, default: "sweeping" do
      transition "Check"    => "sweeping",  from: "sweeping"
      transition "Waive"    => "sweeping",  from: "sweeping"
      transition "Conclude" => "concluded", from: "sweeping"
      transition "Abandon"  => "abandoned", from: "sweeping"
    end

    command "Open" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Begin a pass over one chapter"

      reference_to Target
      attribute :reference, SweepReference
      attribute :engineer,  Engineer

      emits "SweepOpened"
    end

    command "Check" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Put one thing to the system, saying what it should do before looking"

      reference_to Sweep
      attribute :subject,     Subject
      attribute :expectation, Expectation

      # `sequence` IS ABSENT ON PURPOSE — `MutationApplier#entity_element`
      # mints it as `size + 1`. A sweep numbers its checks in the order it
      # made them, which is the only order that could be right.
      #
      # `observation` IS ABSENT FOR THE RULE'S SAKE. A check is written down
      # when it is MADE, carrying only what it expected; what happened arrives
      # afterwards. Allowing both halves in one dispatch would let a check be
      # invented after the fact with its expectation written to match — the
      # one thing this record must make impossible, since it is also the
      # easiest mistake to make in good faith.
      sets :checks, append: { subject: :subject, expectation: :expectation }
      sets :made,   increment: { value: 1 }

      ensures("checking never loses a check") { made.value == old.made.value + 1 }

      emits "CheckMade"
    end

    # A GATE, WAIVED, AND MARKED SO IT CAN BE COUNTED.
    #
    # Every rule in this chapter bends except one. The point is not the escape
    # — it is that `Sweep.Waived` can be read afterwards, so going round the
    # protocol has a price that shows up in a number.
    command "Waive" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Go round a gate, on the record"

      reference_to Sweep
      attribute :reason, WaiverReason

      sets :waived, increment: { value: 1 }

      ensures("a waiver is never silent") { waived.value == old.waived.value + 1 }

      emits "GateWaived"
    end

    command "Conclude" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "End a pass and say what it taught"

      reference_to Sweep
      attribute :notes, SweepNotes

      # THE GATE THE COUNTER EXISTS FOR — and the `waived` clause is what
      # makes it a gate rather than a wall. A sweep that checked nothing
      # tested nothing, and concluding it would put a pass in the record that
      # a later reader takes as evidence the chapter was examined.
      given("a sweep that checked nothing tested nothing") { made.value.positive? || waived.value.positive? }

      sets :notes

      emits "SweepConcluded"
    end

    command "Abandon" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "End a pass whose output should not be trusted"

      reference_to Sweep
      emits "SweepAbandoned"
    end

    query "Sweeping" do
      description "Passes live right now — one per agent, and the reason a target shows as held."
      where(status: "sweeping")
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "Waived" do
      description "Sweeps that went round a gate. Read as a fraction of all of them: a practice whose waiver rate is climbing is not moving faster, it is agreeing with itself less."
      where(:"waived.value" => { gt: 0 })
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "All" do
      description "Every pass ever made — what a runner mints the next reference from."
      order_by :reference
    end
  end

  # ── the bug ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  # ONE FAILING TEST, ONE BUG — AND THE TEST COMES FIRST.
  #
  # Nineteen red examples are not nineteen bugs, and they are not one bug with
  # nineteen failures hanging off it. You take one, write the test that shows
  # it, and log that. If fixing it clears the other sixteen, that is a good
  # day and nobody writes it down.
  #
  # WHICH IS WHY THERE IS NO `found` STATE AND NO `Failure` RECORD. `Log`
  # takes the demonstration as a required argument, so a bug that cannot be
  # shown cannot be written down. It is the one rule here with no way through,
  # because without it there is no evidence.
  aggregate "Bug" do
    description "One thing now known to be wrong, the test that proves it, and how far the fix got."

    reference_to Sweep

    identified_by :reference

    attribute :reference,     BugReference
    attribute :sequence,      BugSequence
    attribute :title,         BugTitle
    attribute :demonstration, Demonstration
    attribute :symptom,       Symptom
    attribute :expectation,   Expectation
    # WHO SAYS SO. A bug with no submitter is an anonymous accusation, and
    # in a ledger more than one agent writes to, "who found this" is the
    # first question anybody asks about a finding they did not make.
    #
    # DISTINCT FROM `held_by`, which is who is working on it NOW and changes
    # every time a claim is taken or goes stale. A submitter never changes:
    # it is a fact about the REPORT, not about the work.
    attribute :submitter,     Submitter
    attribute :order,         BugOrder
    attribute :tags,          list_of(Tag)
    attribute :site,          BugSite
    attribute :cause,         BugCause
    attribute :commit,        CommitRef
    attribute :verification,  Verification
    attribute :reason,        BugReason
    attribute :next_step,     NextStep
    attribute :waived,        WaiverCount
    # THE SAME SIGNATURE `Sweep.waived_by` CARRIES, for the same reason —
    # read that attribute's own comment. Absent until a person waives.
    attribute :waived_by,     WaivedBy
    # SELF-CONTAINED OR BIGGER — the one judgment "On a surprising check"
    # always asked an agent to make and never asked it to WRITE DOWN.
    # `undecided` is the state a bug is born in; `Triage` is the only
    # thing that moves it, and `BugsByDisposition` is what makes the
    # practice's own split ("how much of what we find can we fix on the
    # spot") a number rather than a feeling. `one_of` with a default, the
    # `Check.ever_surprised` idiom, rather than a Boolean this chapter has
    # no precedent for — and `default:` is what makes this ADDITIVE to
    # every bug already on file: each hydrates `undecided`, which is
    # exactly true of a bug nobody triaged.
    attribute :disposition,   one_of("undecided", "self_contained", "bigger"), default: { value: "undecided" }
    # A BUG IS WORK, AND WORK GETS CLAIMED.
    #
    # Sweeping is not the only way an agent gets something to do — it can
    # take the next open bug off the queue and fix that instead, which is
    # most of what a second agent does once a first has been round the
    # chapters. Two agents on one bug is the same waste two agents on one
    # chapter is, so it wants the same protection.
    #
    # NOT A LIFECYCLE HERE, where `Target` uses one. A target's whole state IS
    # whose turn it is; a bug's state is how far the fix got, and holding it
    # is orthogonal to that — an agent picks up an `investigating` bug
    # without moving it. So the race is a `given` rather than an edge, and
    # the serialisation is the same either way: one dispatch per record wins.
    attribute :held_by,       Engineer
    attribute :claimed_at,    Instant
    attribute :window,        StaleAfter

    value_object "BugReference" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a bug is referenced") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "BugSequence" do
      attribute :value, Integer
      invariant("a bug sequence is positive") { value.positive? }
    end

    # PREFIXED, AND THE PREFIX IS LOAD-BEARING. `Facade::Surface.install` puts
    # every aggregate name on Object as a top-level constant, so a value
    # object sharing a word with any aggregate anywhere resolves to that class
    # once the other domain has booted — green alone, red in the suite.
    value_object "BugTitle" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a bug is titled") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # THE TEST THAT PROVES IT. Required at `Log`, which is what makes this
    # chapter's one absolute rule absolute: an argument is satisfied or
    # refused, never waived.
    value_object "Demonstration" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a bug is proven by a test somebody can re-run") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "Symptom" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a bug says what actually happened") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "Expectation" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a bug says what should have happened") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "BugSite" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("an investigation names where the bug lives") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "BugCause" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("an investigation says why the code is wrong") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # 7 TO 40 HEX. A fix that lives in a working tree is not a fix, and a
    # commit is the cheapest possible proof that it does not.
    value_object "CommitRef" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '^[0-9a-fA-F]{7,40}$'
    end

    # WHAT WAS ACTUALLY RUN — "rspec --order random, 1335 examples, 0
    # failures, seed 12345". A count and a seed are checkable; "tests pass" is
    # not, which is the lesson a commit claiming a fix taught this repository
    # by taking nineteen of twenty-seven pizzas examples red.
    value_object "Verification" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a verification says what was actually run") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "BugReason" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("stopping on a bug says why") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # WHO DECIDES, AND WHAT WOULD UNBLOCK IT. A pause without one of these is
    # how "deferred" becomes "forgotten".
    value_object "NextStep" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("stopping on a bug says what would move it") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "WaiverCount" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("a waiver count is not negative") { !value.negative? }
    end

    value_object "WaiverReason" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a waived gate says why") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # REDECLARED, NOT SHARED — this file's own habit (`Engineer`, `Instant`,
    # `CommitRef`); `Sweep::WaivedBy`'s own comment gives the reasoning
    # once for both. The `"qa_sweep"` literal is pinned equal to
    # `QualityControlDials::AUTOMATED_ENGINEER` by spec, here too.
    value_object "WaivedBy" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a gate is waived by a person, never by the loop") { value != "qa_sweep" }
      invariant("a waiver is signed") { value != "nobody" }
    end

    value_object "SweepRef" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "Engineer" do
      attribute :value, String, default: "nobody"
    end

    value_object "Instant" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("an instant is not before the epoch") { !value.negative? }
    end

    value_object "StaleAfter" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 900
      invariant("a staleness window is positive") { value.positive? }
    end

    # TAGS ARE A SET ON THE BUG, NOT AN ENTITY UNDER IT — and the choice is
    # about what a tag IS. An entity has identity, a lifecycle and a history:
    # a check has all three, which is why a check is one. A tag has none. It
    # is a word that is either on the bug or not, nobody cares when it was
    # put there, and "flaky" on one bug is the same "flaky" as on another.
    #
    # THE COST IS THAT TAGGING REPLACES RATHER THAN APPENDS. There is no
    # append in this language — `sets` sets — so `Tag` takes the whole
    # set and the caller sends every tag it wants the bug to end up with.
    # That reads worse in one place and better everywhere else: removing a
    # tag needs no second verb, tagging twice is not two tags, and the
    # attribute is always exactly what the bug is tagged with.
    # A SUBMITTER IS SOMEBODY, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT AN `Engineer`.
    #
    # `Engineer` defaults to "nobody" because `held_by` needs that value — an
    # unclaimed bug IS held by nobody, and the default is what makes the claim
    # arithmetic work. Reusing the type here gave `submitter` the same default
    # and made it optional in everything but intent: omit it and the ledger
    # cheerfully recorded that nobody found this. `--help` said so out loud —
    # `submitter.value  String; defaults to "nobody"` — which is how it was
    # caught.
    #
    # So it is its own type, with no default and an invariant that says the
    # quiet part. The two fields look alike and mean opposite things: one is
    # who is working on it NOW and changes constantly, the other is who
    # reported it and never changes at all.
    value_object "Submitter" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'

      invariant("a bug is reported by somebody") { value != "nobody" }
    end

    # WHAT TO DO FIRST. The queue was ordered by `sequence` — the order things
    # were FOUND in, which is a fact about the sweep and says nothing about
    # what matters. An order is somebody's judgement, and it is kept apart from
    # the discovery order so that changing your mind about priority does not
    # rewrite the history of when a thing was seen.
    #
    # LOWER IS SOONER, and the default is deliberately not zero: a bug nobody
    # has ranked sits BEHIND everything anybody has, rather than jumping the
    # whole queue by saying nothing.
    value_object "BugOrder" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 1_000

      invariant("an order is a position, not a direction") { !value.negative? }
    end

    value_object "Tag" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: "^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]*$"

      invariant("a tag is a word, not a sentence") { !value.empty? }
    end

    # A bug is born PROVEN. There is no state for "somebody thinks something
    # is wrong" — that is a check, and it lives on the sweep.
    lifecycle :status, default: "logged" do
      transition "Investigate" => "investigating", from: "logged"
      transition "Fix"         => "fixed",         from: "investigating"
      transition "Verify"      => "verified",      from: "fixed"
      transition "Pause"       => "paused",        from: ["logged", "investigating"]
      # THE TEST CAN BE WRONG — not the bug, the test. `withdrawn` is for a
      # report that proved something other than what it claimed, and it is a
      # different fact from a fix that worked.
      transition "Withdraw"    => "withdrawn",     from: ["logged", "investigating"]
      transition "Regress"     => "investigating", from: ["fixed", "verified"]
      transition "Revisit"     => "investigating", from: "paused"
    end

    command "Log" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Write down something that is wrong, with the test that proves it"

      reference_to Sweep
      attribute :reference,     BugReference
      attribute :sequence,      BugSequence
      attribute :title,         BugTitle
      attribute :demonstration, Demonstration
      attribute :symptom,       Symptom
      attribute :expectation,   Expectation
      # REQUIRED, LIKE THE DEMONSTRATION. Both answer "why should anybody
      # believe this" — one is the evidence, the other is who produced it,
      # and a ledger that let either be omitted would be collecting claims
      # rather than findings.
      attribute :submitter,     Submitter
      attribute :tags,          list_of(Tag), optional: true

      emits "BugLogged"
    end

    # TAGGING IS NOT A LIFECYCLE MOVE, deliberately. A bug can be tagged
    # while logged, while being fixed, after it is verified, after it is
    # withdrawn — a tag says what KIND of thing this is, and that is knowable
    # at any point and does not change what may happen next. Putting it in
    # the lifecycle would mean inventing a state per tag, which is how a
    # lifecycle stops describing the work.
    # NOT A LIFECYCLE MOVE, for the same reason `Tag` is not: a bug can be
    # ranked while logged, while being fixed, or after it is paused, and
    # ranking it changes nothing about what may happen to it next.
    command "Rank" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Say what should be dealt with first"

      reference_to Bug
      attribute :order, BugOrder

      sets :order

      emits "BugRanked"
    end

    command "Tag" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Say what kind of thing this bug is, so it can be found with its kind later"

      reference_to Bug
      attribute :tags, list_of(Tag)

      sets :tags

      emits "BugTagged"
    end

    # THE LOOKUP THE TAGS EXIST FOR. `contains` against a bound argument
    # rather than a literal — banking's `Flagged` asks the same question with
    # `"high_risk"` written into the chapter, which answers one question
    # forever; this asks whichever one you have.
    # EVERYTHING ONE AGENT FOUND, whatever became of it. The counterpart of
    # `InHand`, which answers what somebody is holding NOW: this answers what
    # they have ever reported, which is the question a daily report is
    # written from and the one a second agent asks before trusting a queue.
    query "From" do
      description "Every bug one submitter has ever logged, in whatever state — what an agent found, as against what it is holding."
      attribute :submitter, Submitter
      where(submitter: :submitter)
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "Tagged" do
      description "Every bug carrying one tag, whatever state it is in — the way to find all the flaky ones, or everything about the projector."
      attribute :tag, Tag
      where(tags: { contains: :tag })
      order_by :reference
    end

    # THE SAME RULE AS A TARGET'S, IN THE SAME ARITHMETIC. Held by nobody, or
    # held long enough ago that the holder has gone quiet. `+` rather than `-`
    # because that is the operator the sublanguage has, and `now` arrives as
    # an argument because a predicate cannot ask the time.
    command "Claim" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Take the next bug off the queue, or one whose holder has gone quiet"

      # SAME RULE, SAME REASON — see Target.Claim. The window is the practice's,
      # on the record, not the claimer's to name.
      reference_to Bug
      attribute :held_by, Engineer
      attribute :now,     Instant

      given("a live claim is not taken from the agent holding it") do
        held_by.value == "nobody" || claimed_at.value + window.value <= now.value
      end

      sets :held_by
      sets :claimed_at, to: :now

      emits "BugClaimed"
    end

    command "Drop" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Put a bug back on the queue without finishing it"

      reference_to Bug
      attribute :held_by, Engineer

      sets :held_by

      emits "BugDropped"
    end

    command "Investigate" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Name the code that is wrong and say why"

      reference_to Bug
      attribute :site,  BugSite
      attribute :cause, BugCause

      sets :site
      sets :cause

      emits "BugInvestigated"
    end

    # `reference` IS DECLARED HERE, RESTATED — the one attribute on this
    # command that names something the caller already said by addressing
    # the bug at all. Redundant on its face; load-bearing underneath.
    #
    # A mutating command's emitted event carries exactly the arguments it
    # was dispatched WITH — never the record's own identity, which travels
    # as routing (`to:`/`id:`), not as a fact (`CommandRules::Emission#emit`:
    # `payload: args`, nothing merged in from the hydrated instance). So
    # `BugFixed` said `commit` and nothing else, and `BugCiWatch` below —
    # the process manager that starts on this exact event — had no way to
    # remember WHICH bug it was watching for: a saga's own memory is a copy
    # of the starting event's payload and nothing more (`SagaInterpreter
    # #begin_saga`), so an absent field here is an absent field for the
    # rest of that saga's life, silently, which is the one failure mode
    # this whole chapter exists to hunt.
    #
    # The fix is the same shape banking's own `Settlement` already leans
    # on for `Account::Debit`'s `reference:` — a scalar restated purely so
    # it rides the event forward — except EXPLICIT here rather than
    # smuggled through the argument gate's own domain-wide `correlation_
    # keys` widening (that mechanism's own comment calls itself "the
    # weakest part of the gate"): a caller who forgets `reference:` is
    # refused outright (`AbsentArgument`), not silently short a field three
    # steps downstream.
    command "Fix" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Record the commit that makes it stop happening"

      reference_to Bug
      attribute :reference, BugReference
      attribute :commit,    CommitRef

      sets :commit

      emits "BugFixed"
    end

    command "Verify" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Record that the fix was actually verified, and exactly how"

      reference_to Bug
      attribute :evidence, Verification

      sets :verification, to: :evidence

      emits "BugVerified"
    end

    command "Pause" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Stop on a bug that is real and too large to fix here — why, and what would move it"

      reference_to Bug
      attribute :reason,    BugReason
      attribute :next_step, NextStep

      sets :reason
      sets :next_step

      emits "BugPaused"
    end

    command "Withdraw" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Take back a report whose test turned out to prove something else"

      reference_to Bug
      attribute :reason, BugReason

      sets :reason

      emits "BugWithdrawn"
    end

    command "Regress" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Put a bug back when its fix stopped holding"

      reference_to Bug
      emits "BugRegressed"
    end

    command "Revisit" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Pick a paused bug back up, investigation intact"

      reference_to Bug
      emits "BugRevisited"
    end

    command "Waive" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Go round a gate on this bug, on the record"

      reference_to Bug
      attribute :reason, WaiverReason

      sets :waived, increment: { value: 1 }

      emits "GateWaived"
    end

    query "Open" do
      description "Every bug still owed work — the open ledger, derived rather than maintained."
      where(status: { in: "logged,investigating,paused" })
      order_by :sequence
    end

    # THE OTHER WAY TO GET WORK. Sweeping finds new bugs; this is the pile of
    # ones already found and not yet in anybody's hands, oldest first — which
    # is what an agent that is not sweeping picks from.
    query "Queue" do
      description "Open bugs nobody is holding, most important first, then oldest. The queue an agent takes from when it is fixing rather than sweeping."
      where(status: { in: "logged,investigating" })
      where(:"held_by.value" => "nobody")
      # RANK FIRST, THEN AGE. Ordering by `sequence` alone made the queue the
      # order things were found in, which is the one ordering nobody wants
      # from a queue. Age stays as the tiebreak so two bugs nobody has ranked
      # still come out oldest-first rather than arbitrarily.
      # THE SCALAR, NOT THE VALUE OBJECT. `order_by :order` sorted by the
      # whole `{value: n}` and put the bug ranked 1 BEHIND two ranked 1000 —
      # silently, in the one query whose entire job is the ordering. A VO is
      # ordered by whatever comparing two of them happens to do; the number
      # inside it is what anybody meant.
      order_by :"order.value"
    end

    query "InHand" do
      description "Open bugs somebody is holding, and since when. A claim older than the window is free for the taking — read claimed_at before assuming somebody is on it."
      where(status: { in: "logged,investigating" })
      where(:"held_by.value" => { ne: "nobody" })
      order_by :claimed_at
    end

    query "FoundIn" do
      description "Everything one sweep turned up, in the order it was found."
      attribute :sweep_id, SweepRef
      where(sweep: :sweep_id)
      order_by :sequence
    end

    query "ReadyToVerify" do
      description "Fixed and not yet re-run — the queue a full CI pass empties."
      where(status: "fixed")
      order_by :sequence
    end

    query "Paused" do
      description "Real, unfixed, stopped on, each with what would move it. The only queue a ticket is ever raised from."
      where(status: "paused")
      order_by :sequence
    end

    query "Withdrawn" do
      description "Reports whose test proved something other than what was claimed. Read the reasons: a run of these is a practice testing the wrong thing, not a runtime behaving."
      where(status: "withdrawn")
      order_by :sequence
    end

    query "Waived" do
      description "Bugs that went round one of their own gates — the honesty check on any claim about following the protocol."
      where(:"waived.value" => { gt: 0 })
      order_by :sequence
    end

    query "All" do
      description "Every bug ever logged — what a runner mints the next reference from."
      order_by :sequence
    end
  end

  # ── where to look next ───────────────────────────────────────────────

  # THE PRACTICE'S OWN MEMORY OF WHERE TO LOOK, KEPT AS DATA — the
  # "stored-prompt-corpus" idea, given a real shape. Every sweep so far has
  # found its bugs by an agent's own judgement, re-derived from scratch each
  # session: read the recent commits, read the open PRs, guess at what
  # hasn't been tried yet. That works, but it does not ACCUMULATE — the
  # angle a session considered and set aside lives in that session's own
  # transcript, which the next session cannot query and usually never even
  # sees. This aggregate is what lets "which construct combination hasn't
  # been fuzzed yet" survive the session that first thought of it.
  #
  # AN AGGREGATE, NOT A THIRD BARE CONSTANT NEXT TO `QualityControlDials`.
  # The dials comment at the top of this file explains why a live,
  # dispatchable record was tried and taken BACK for those two numbers — but
  # that reasoning is specific to them, not a blanket rule against
  # aggregates for practice-level bookkeeping. `CADENCE_SECONDS` and
  # `PR_CAP_PER_DAY` are each ONE fact, fixed until a human edits the file,
  # with nothing that happens during a sweep ever changing either. An angle
  # is the opposite of that on every count that matters: there are MANY of
  # them, each with its own real state that moves on its own — proposed,
  # picked up, built or set aside — and that movement is exactly the
  # history a future sweep needs to read before inventing its own from
  # scratch. A bare constant cannot hold a growing list of records each
  # with their own lifecycle at all; this is precisely the shape `Bug`
  # already is, aimed one step earlier, at what to go looking for rather
  # than what was already found.
  #
  # A "LEAD," NOT A TASK. `Angle` deliberately does not say what to BUILD —
  # `citation` says what already-known fact makes it worth chasing, and
  # `resolution` (once one exists) says what actually got built from it.
  # The gap between the two is real work an agent still has to do; this
  # record only refuses to let that work start from nothing.
  aggregate "Angle" do
    description "One concrete, citable reason to suspect an uninvestigated corner exists — proposed, chased down, and either built into something real or set aside with a reason. The practice's own backlog of what to try next, so a future sweep mines its own history instead of guessing."

    identified_by :reference

    attribute :reference,   AngleReference
    attribute :premise,     AnglePremise
    attribute :citation,    AngleCitation
    attribute :proposer,    Proposer
    attribute :proposed_at, Instant
    attribute :resolution,  AngleResolution
    attribute :reason,      AngleReason

    value_object "AngleReference" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("an angle is referenced") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # SUBSTANCE, ENFORCED THE WAY `SweepNotes` ALREADY ENFORCES IT — a
    # number in the invariant's own sentence, because the sentence IS the
    # refusal. "look at sagas more" is a mood, not a lead: it gives a
    # future reader nothing to act on that they could not have guessed
    # themselves. Sixty is not a magic number so much as a tripwire against
    # exactly that one-line stub — long enough that a genuine restating of
    # what was checked and what remains cannot fit under it by accident.
    value_object "AnglePremise" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("an angle says enough to be acted on without its proposer in the room, in at least 60 characters") { value.to_s.size >= 60 }
    end

    # WHAT MAKES THIS A LEAD AND NOT A HUNCH. A Bug reference, an ADR
    # number, or a named construct-combination gap — something another
    # reader can go and look at themselves, the same distinction
    # `Demonstration` already draws for a bug: a claim nobody can
    # independently check is not evidence, it is an opinion with a period
    # after it.
    value_object "AngleCitation" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("an angle cites something concrete, not a mood") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # NOT `Engineer` — the exact reasoning `Bug`'s own `Submitter` already
    # gives for the identical split: `held_by`-shaped types default to
    # "nobody" because an unclaimed record legitimately IS held by nobody,
    # and reusing that default here would let an angle's own authorship go
    # unrecorded the same quiet way a bug's once did, until `--help` said
    # so out loud.
    value_object "Proposer" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("an angle is proposed by somebody") { value != "nobody" }
    end

    value_object "Instant" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("an instant is not before the epoch") { !value.negative? }
    end

    # WHAT ACTUALLY CAME OF IT — a Target reference, a PR number, a stress
    # domain's own path, whatever a later reader would need to go confirm
    # the lead was real, not merely closed. Set once, at `Build`, and only
    # then: an angle cannot claim a resolution it does not yet have, the
    # same way a `Bug` cannot be `Fix`ed before it is `Investigate`d.
    value_object "AngleResolution" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a built angle says what actually got built") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # THE SAME PATTERN `Target::Shelve` ALREADY ESTABLISHED, restated here
    # rather than shared across aggregates: deciding NOT to chase a lead is
    # a real decision with its own reasoning, and a discarded angle with no
    # reason recorded is indistinguishable from one nobody ever looked at.
    value_object "AngleReason" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("discarding an angle says why") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # PROPOSED, THEN INVESTIGATING, THEN ONE OF TWO ENDINGS — the exact
    # shape `Bug` already uses for logged/investigating/fixed, deliberately:
    # `Build` is only reachable FROM `investigating`, so a lead cannot be
    # marked built without ever having been picked up first, the same
    # discipline that keeps a bug from being `Fix`ed before it is
    # `Investigate`d. `Discard` reads from both live states — like
    # `Target::Shelve`'s own `from: ["waiting", "held"]` — because a lead
    # can turn out not worth the time either before anyone starts (a
    # citation that turns out to already be covered) or partway through
    # (real investigation finds the angle is a dead end after all).
    lifecycle :status, default: "proposed" do
      transition "Investigate" => "investigating", from: "proposed"
      transition "Build"       => "built",         from: "investigating"
      transition "Discard"     => "discarded",     from: ["proposed", "investigating"]
    end

    command "Propose" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Write down a concrete, citable reason to suspect an uninvestigated corner exists, before it is lost to a conversation"

      attribute :reference, AngleReference
      attribute :premise,   AnglePremise
      attribute :citation,  AngleCitation
      attribute :proposer,  Proposer
      # OMITTED ARGUMENT, FILLED FROM THE CLOCK — `now:` reaches here
      # exactly the way `Target.Claim`'s own comment already explains: a
      # predicate cannot ask the time, but a plain assignment does not need
      # to, and the same `clock` port that already answers `target.claim`
      # answers this the identical way, including believing an explicit
      # value over the clock when a caller supplies one.
      attribute :now,       Instant

      sets :proposed_at, to: :now

      emits "AngleProposed"
    end

    command "Investigate" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Pick a lead up for a real look, rather than leaving it to look picked-up by default"

      reference_to Angle
      emits "AngleInvestigated"
    end

    command "Build" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Say what actually came of chasing this lead"

      reference_to Angle
      attribute :resolution, AngleResolution

      sets :resolution

      emits "AngleBuilt"
    end

    command "Discard" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Decide a lead is not worth the practice's time, and say why"

      reference_to Angle
      attribute :reason, AngleReason

      sets :reason

      emits "AngleDiscarded"
    end

    # THE BACKLOG — the way `Bug.Queue` is the backlog an agent not
    # sweeping picks from, this is the backlog an agent not sure WHAT to
    # sweep reads first. Oldest first: `order_by :"proposed_at.value"`
    # rather than `order_by :proposed_at`, for the exact reason `Bug.Queue`'s
    # own comment already gives about `BugOrder` — ordering by the whole
    # value object sorts by however two of them happen to compare, not by
    # the number a reader actually means.
    query "Backlog" do
      description "Leads nobody has finished chasing yet, oldest first — read this before inventing a new angle from scratch."
      where(status: { in: "proposed,investigating" })
      order_by :"proposed_at.value"
    end

    # THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME LIST — what became of every earlier lead,
    # so a reader sees history rather than only the live backlog. Built and
    # discarded sit in one query on purpose: a discarded angle is not a
    # failure to hide, it is evidence a citation was already covered, and
    # burying it in a separate, less-read query would make the practice
    # look more decisive than it actually was.
    query "Resolved" do
      description "Leads already acted on, one way or the other — built into something real, or discarded with a reason. Read beside Backlog for the practice's own hit rate."
      where(status: { in: "built,discarded" })
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "All" do
      description "Every angle ever proposed — what a runner mints the next reference from."
      order_by :reference
    end
  end

  # ── the outside world ────────────────────────────────────────────────

  # RAISE AN ISSUE ONLY FOR A BUG YOU COULD NOT FIX YOURSELF.
  #
  # `Raise` requires `reference_to Bug`, and that reference has to resolve at
  # dispatch — the strongest form a rule takes in this language. What it
  # cannot check is the bug's STATE, because a reference is an id and not the
  # record it names; `RestingOnUnpaused` below is where that half is surfaced.
  aggregate "Ticket" do
    description "One issue raised in a tracker outside this repository — what it says, whether the tracker took it, and how many times we have asked."

    reference_to Bug

    identified_by :reference

    attribute :reference,  TicketReference
    attribute :repository, TicketRepository
    attribute :title,      TicketTitle
    attribute :body,         TicketBody
    attribute :pull_request, PullRequest
    attribute :number,     IssueNumber
    attribute :url,        IssueUrl
    attribute :refusal,    RefusalText
    attribute :attempts,   AttemptCount
    # THE CAP LIVES ON THE RECORD, not on the retry command. A policy carries
    # the event payload and nothing else, so a `max` passed as an argument is
    # a `max` the retry never receives — the reaction is refused with "Retry
    # was not given max" and the ticket stops dead one attempt in. Banking's
    # ScheduledPayment holds `max_attempts` for exactly this reason.
    attribute :max_attempts, MaxAttempts

    value_object "TicketReference" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a ticket is referenced") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "TicketRepository" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$'
    end

    value_object "TicketTitle" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a ticket is titled") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # A FIX MAY ALREADY EXIST, AND THE TICKET SHOULD SAY SO.
    #
    # Filing an issue for a bug somebody has already opened a pull request for
    # is how the same work gets done twice. The URL is on the TICKET rather
    # than only in the body prose because the ledger should be able to ANSWER
    # "which of these already have a fix proposed" — `Ticket.Proposed` below —
    # and a substring search of prose is not an answer.
    #
    # OPTIONAL, because most tickets are raised before anybody has fixed
    # anything. An empty one is the ordinary case, not a missing value, which
    # is why it defaults rather than being refused.
    value_object "PullRequest" do
      attribute :value, String, default: ""
    end

    value_object "TicketBody" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a ticket says something") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "IssueNumber" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("an issue number is not negative") { !value.negative? }
    end

    value_object "IssueUrl" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "RefusalText" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # THREE TRIES, AND THEN A HUMAN. The cap lives on the record rather than
    # in the retry policy, because a policy carries no state and would ask
    # forever — banking's `ScheduledPayment` holds `attempts` for exactly this
    # reason and `Retry`'s own given reads it.
    value_object "AttemptCount" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("an attempt count is not negative") { !value.negative? }
    end

    value_object "MaxAttempts" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 3
      invariant("a filing is attempted at least once") { value.positive? }
    end

    value_object "BugRef" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # RAISING AND SENDING ARE TWO VERBS, and the split is not ceremony.
    #
    # A crash between them leaves a row in `Ticket.Raised` — composed, not
    # sent — rather than a record claiming a filing that never went out. And
    # it is what makes the ask drivable at all: a policy re-enters with the
    # EVENT payload, a creating command emits its value objects, and a
    # reference slot takes an id and refuses an object. `Submit` is
    # non-creating, so `TicketSubmitted` carries `{ id: … }`, which fits.
    lifecycle :status, default: "raised" do
      transition "Submit"  => "submitting", from: ["raised", "refused"]
      transition "Filed"   => "filed",      from: "submitting"
      transition "Refused" => "refused",    from: "submitting"
      transition "Retry"   => "submitting", from: "refused"
      transition "Abandon" => "abandoned",  from: "refused"
      transition "Close"   => "closed",     from: "filed"
    end

    # THE PORT IS DECLARED IN THE HECKSAGON, NOT HERE — see
    # `quality_control.hecksagon`. That is where pizzas puts its own
    # `PaymentGateway` too, and the reason is that a port is where the domain
    # meets a swappable adapter: its SHAPE is vocabulary, but its existence is
    # a wiring decision, and wiring lives with the binds.
    #
    # What it says there: `asks "File"`, naming `IssueFiled` and
    # `IssueFilingRefused` as its two endings, and `tells "Closed"` for when
    # somebody closes the issue upstream. The policies at the foot of this
    # chapter are what drive it.

    command "Raise" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Say the outside world needs to know about a bug we could not fix"

      reference_to Bug
      attribute :reference,    TicketReference
      attribute :repository,   TicketRepository
      attribute :title,        TicketTitle
      attribute :body,         TicketBody
      # CARRIED INTO THE FILING WITHOUT A SECOND VERB. `held_state` hands the
      # whole ticket to the adapter, so a field on the record reaches `gh`
      # even though the ask itself declares no argument for it.
      attribute :pull_request, PullRequest, optional: true

      emits "TicketRaised"
    end

    # DISPATCH THIS WITH `id:`, NOT THROUGH THE FACADE.
    #
    # `TicketSubmitted` is what the policy re-enters the port with, verbatim,
    # and a port's reference slot takes an id and deliberately refuses an
    # object. `Handle#run` — the facade form, `ticket.submit` — addresses a
    # record by its IDENTITY HEADS, so the payload carries `reference` as a
    # TicketReference and the ask is refused with "File was not given id".
    # Dispatched as `Ticket.Submit(id: "TK-1")` the payload is `{ id: … }`,
    # which is what the port wants.
    #
    # A real rough edge, and worth naming here rather than letting somebody
    # rediscover it: the two ways of addressing the same record produce
    # different payloads, and only one of them can drive an ask.
    command "Submit" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Send it — the last reversible moment"

      reference_to Ticket
      emits "TicketSubmitted"
    end

    command "Filed" do
      role "System"
      goal "Record that the tracker took it, and where it landed"

      reference_to Ticket
      attribute :number, IssueNumber
      attribute :url,    IssueUrl

      sets :number
      sets :url

      emits "TicketFiled"
    end

    command "Refused" do
      role "System"
      goal "Record that the tracker would not take it, and what it said"

      reference_to Ticket
      attribute :refusal, RefusalText

      sets :refusal
      sets :attempts, increment: { value: 1 }

      emits "TicketFilingRefused"
    end

    # `refusal` IS TAKEN AND CARRIED FORWARD, and it is not decoration.
    #
    # A policy re-enters its target with the emitting event's payload
    # VERBATIM, and `TicketFilingRefused` carries the refusal. A `Retry`
    # declaring no arguments is refused outright — "Retry does not declare
    # refusal" — and the ticket stops dead one attempt in, with the reaction
    # log the only place saying so. So the target of a policy must accept
    # exactly what the event carries.
    #
    # Given it has to be accepted, it is SET rather than ignored: an argument
    # taken and dropped is the precise smell this chapter exists to hunt, and
    # writing one into the very record that hunts it would be a poor joke.
    # Carrying it forward is also true — a retry knows what it is retrying
    # after.
    command "Retry" do
      role "System"
      goal "Ask again, up to the limit, carrying what was refused"

      reference_to Ticket
      attribute :refusal, RefusalText

      given("a retry is still allowed") { attempts.value < max_attempts.value }

      sets :refusal

      emits "TicketRetried"
    end

    command "Abandon" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Stop asking, and put it in front of a person"

      reference_to Ticket
      emits "TicketFilingAbandoned"
    end

    command "Close" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Record that the issue is done with"

      reference_to Ticket
      emits "TicketClosed"
    end

    # WHICH ONES ALREADY HAVE A FIX. The reason the URL is a field and not
    # prose: somebody triaging a list of filed tickets wants to skip the ones
    # already answered, and that has to be a question the ledger can take.
    query "Proposed" do
      description "Tickets that already carry a pull request — a fix exists, so more work filed against them is duplicated effort."
      where(:"pull_request.value" => { ne: "" })
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "Raised" do
      description "Composed and not sent. Nothing will happen to these until somebody submits one."
      where(status: "raised")
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "Submitting" do
      description "Sent and not yet answered. A row that sticks here is a call whose outcome nobody recorded — look at the tracker before asking again, or a retry files a duplicate."
      where(status: "submitting")
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "Filed" do
      description "Open issues this practice is responsible for, out in the world."
      where(status: "filed")
      order_by :number
    end

    query "Refused" do
      description "The tracker said no, and what it said. Retried automatically until the cap."
      where(status: "refused")
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "Abandoned" do
      description "Asked three times, refused three times. Every row belongs in the report, because nothing automatic will touch it again."
      where(status: "abandoned")
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "ForBug" do
      description "Every ticket ever raised about one bug — the duplicate check, before a second is raised."
      attribute :bug_id, BugRef
      where(bug: :bug_id)
      order_by :reference
    end

    # A DOTTED WHERE, HOPPING A REFERENCE — because the rule it carries cannot
    # be a `given`. "Only raise a ticket for a bug you could not fix" reads
    # like a precondition, but `bug.status` resolves to nothing inside a
    # predicate; only a where-clause hops. So it is surfaced, not enforced,
    # which is honest: raising ahead of the pause is a mistake, not a crime.
    query "RestingOnUnpaused" do
      description "Tickets about a bug nobody has stopped on. Raise an issue only for something you could not fix yourself — this is the list where that gets skipped."
      where(status: { in: "raised,filed" })
      where(:"bug/status" => { in: "logged,investigating" })
      order_by :reference
    end

    query "RestingOnFixed" do
      description "Tickets about a bug this repository went on to fix itself. Each one owes the outside world a Close."
      where(status: "filed")
      where(:"bug/status" => { in: "fixed,verified" })
      order_by :number
    end

    query "All" do
      description "Every ticket ever raised."
      order_by :reference
    end
  end

  # ── which pull requests are ours ─────────────────────────────────────

  # THE THING THIS WHOLE AGGREGATE EXISTS TO END: `bin/qa_pr_check` used to
  # ask GitHub to GUESS which PRs were ours — `gh pr list --search`,
  # matching a branch prefix (`loop-parity/*`) or a title convention
  # (`"qa: ..."`). Checked against this practice's own real history, not
  # assumed: neither is a superset of the other. #533/#532/#529/#526 are
  # real `loop-parity/*` branches titled `"heki: ..."`/`"BUG#N: ..."`/
  # `"rust: ..."` — invisible to a title search. #534 is titled `"qa: ..."`
  # on a branch that never got the `loop-parity/` prefix at all — invisible
  # to a branch search. It sat open and genuinely red, unseen by both, for
  # a day. Widening the search to the union of both patterns is still a
  # GUESS about every naming habit an agent will ever have — it only has to
  # be wrong once more.
  #
  # THE FIX IS THE ONE THIS CHAPTER ALREADY MAKES EVERYWHERE ELSE: STOP
  # RE-DERIVING A FACT THAT WAS ALREADY KNOWN, AND WRITE IT DOWN AT THE
  # MOMENT IT WAS. `hecks_qa` knows a PR's number, url, branch and head
  # commit the instant it opens one — that is `gh pr create`'s own return
  # value, not a guess about it afterward. `Open` below records exactly
  # that, once, and `bin/qa_pr_check` from here on reads `Patch.Open` for
  # the worklist and asks `gh` about each one BY ITS OWN NUMBER — the same
  # move `Bug.commit` already made for the identical reason (`BugCiWatch`'s
  # own header explains why that commit has to be exact rather than
  # re-derived), aimed one layer up: not just which commit is ours, but
  # which PR is ours in the first place.
  #
  # NOT NAMED `PullRequest` — `Ticket`, forty lines up, already owns that
  # word. `Ticket::PullRequest` is a value object (the URL a ticket carries
  # once a fix exists for the issue it filed), and `Facade::Surface
  # .install` puts every AGGREGATE name on Object as a top-level constant
  # the identical way `Bug::BugTitle`'s own comment already warns about for
  # the mirror case — a value object sharing a word with an aggregate
  # resolves to whichever one booted last. An aggregate named `PullRequest`
  # here would collide with `Ticket`'s own value object of that name the
  # moment both are loaded, which within this one file they always are.
  # `Patch` says the same thing — a change offered against a Bug's own fix
  # — without reusing a word this chapter already spoke for something else.
  aggregate "Patch" do
    description "One pull request opened for a Bug's own fix — number, branch, and the exact commit that makes checking its CI status a lookup instead of a guess."

    reference_to Bug

    identified_by :number

    attribute :number, PatchNumber
    attribute :url,    PatchUrl
    attribute :branch, PatchBranch
    attribute :commit, CommitRef
    attribute :title,  PatchTitle
    # WHEN, AS A NUMBER — what makes `QualityControlDials::PR_CAP_PER_DAY`
    # COUNTABLE (`OpenedSince`, below; `bin/qa_open_pr` is the reader).
    # `default: 0` for the `CleanStreak` reason: every patch recorded
    # before this field existed hydrates at the epoch, which `OpenedSince`
    # never counts against today's cap — exactly right for a PR opened
    # before the cap was ever enforced.
    attribute :opened_at, Instant

    value_object "Instant" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("an instant is not before the epoch") { !value.negative? }
    end

    value_object "PatchNumber" do
      attribute :value, Integer
      invariant("a patch is numbered") { value.positive? }
    end

    value_object "PatchUrl" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a patch says where it lives") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "PatchBranch" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a patch says which branch it is on") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # THE FIELD THAT RETIRES THE OLD SEARCH. `bin/qa_pr_check` used to have
    # to ask GitHub to find the commit for a PR it only had a guess about;
    # recording it here at `Open` means the commit is in hand the moment a
    # PR needs checking at all — the same "written down before it's
    # needed" discipline `Bug.commit` already keeps for the reason
    # `BugCiWatch`'s own header gives.
    value_object "CommitRef" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '^[0-9a-fA-F]{7,40}$'
    end

    value_object "PatchTitle" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a patch is titled") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "BugRef" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # OPENED, THEN ONE OF TWO ENDINGS GITHUB ITSELF REPORTS — the same
    # `raised` → `filed`/`closed` shape `Ticket`'s own lifecycle already
    # gives an outside-world record, restated here rather than shared
    # (this file's own established habit — `Engineer`, `Instant`,
    # `CommitRef` are each redeclared per aggregate too). Both endings are
    # terminal on purpose: a merged PR does not reopen, and this ledger's
    # memory of a closed one does not either. GitHub's own history is where
    # to look if somehow either did.
    lifecycle :status, default: "opened" do
      transition "Merge" => "merged", from: "opened"
      transition "Close" => "closed", from: "opened"
    end

    # DISPATCHED BY WHOEVER OPENS THE PR — which, per `.claude/skills/
    # hecks_qa/SKILL.md`'s own wiring, is the ORCHESTRATOR, once a sweep
    # subagent's report comes back naming the number, url, branch, commit
    # and title it already has from its own `gh pr create`. Not the
    # subagent itself: see that file's own new step for why this piece of
    # bookkeeping is kept an orchestrator action rather than something
    # handed to a subagent to dispatch on its own.
    command "Open" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Record that a fix has been opened as a pull request, the moment its number, branch and commit are already known"

      reference_to Bug
      attribute :number, PatchNumber
      attribute :url,    PatchUrl
      attribute :branch, PatchBranch
      attribute :commit, CommitRef
      attribute :title,  PatchTitle

      emits "PatchOpened"
    end

    command "Merge" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Record that GitHub merged it"

      reference_to Patch
      emits "PatchMerged"
    end

    command "Close" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Record that GitHub closed it without merging"

      reference_to Patch
      emits "PatchClosed"
    end

    # THE NEW SOURCE OF TRUTH FOR `bin/qa_pr_check` — every PR this ledger
    # believes is still open, to check by ITS OWN number rather than by
    # asking GitHub to search for one that might be ours. Read this
    # instead of `gh pr list --search`, full stop: for each row, ask `gh`
    # about that exact number and nothing else.
    query "Open" do
      description "Every PR we've opened that's still open, by number — bin/qa_pr_check's own worklist. For each of these, ask gh for exactly this number's CI status; nothing here is a guess."
      where(status: "opened")
      order_by :number
    end

    query "ForBug" do
      description "Every patch ever opened for one bug — the duplicate check, the same shape Ticket.ForBug already gives for an issue."
      attribute :bug_id, BugRef
      where(bug: :bug_id)
      order_by :number
    end

    query "All" do
      description "Every patch ever opened — what a runner reads back to reconcile against GitHub's own list."
      order_by :number
    end
  end

  # ── deliberate work, landed ───────────────────────────────────────────

  # NOT EVERY REAL PR IS A BUG'S OWN FIX. This whole practice has opened
  # real PRs that name no `Bug` at all — a backlog aggregate (`Angle`,
  # above), a compaction tool, `Patch` itself — because `Bug` specifically
  # means "one thing now known to be wrong, proven with a failing test"
  # (this file's own header, and `Bug`'s own description). Widening
  # `Patch.Open`'s `reference_to Bug` to accept something else instead —
  # or making it `optional: true` so a patch could name neither — was
  # asked and refused: a bug fix without a `Bug` behind it is exactly the
  # blurring `Bug`'s own meaning exists to prevent, and the repository
  # owner confirmed it stays required, unchanged. So this is a SIBLING,
  # not a second door into `Patch`: its own aggregate, its own lifecycle,
  # its own CI watch below — mirroring `Patch`/`BugCiWatch`'s shape for a
  # different kind of PR rather than teaching either of them a second
  # meaning.
  #
  # `Improvement` — checked against this file's own naming hazard before
  # committing to it, the same way `Patch`'s own comment already checked
  # itself: `Facade::Surface.install` puts every AGGREGATE name in every
  # loaded bluebook onto `Object` as a top-level constant, so a second
  # aggregate anywhere in the corpus sharing this word would resolve to
  # whichever booted last. Grepped for `aggregate "Improvement"` across
  # every `.bluebook` file in this repository (examples/, qa/, the
  # framework chapters, every fixture) — nothing. Not a Ruby core
  # constant either. Plainer synonyms considered and set aside:
  # `Enhancement` (no shorter, no plainer, and this file already prefers
  # the word an engineer would actually say), `Change`/`Build`/`Delivery`
  # (each already a verb or noun doing other work nearby — `Angle.Build`
  # a few hundred lines up is the literal reason `Build` was struck
  # first).
  #
  # NOT SHAPED LIKE `Bug`. `logged → investigating → fixed → verified` is
  # what it costs to prove something is actually broken before touching
  # it; there is no investigation here because nothing is broken — the
  # work was already decided, deliberately, before a line of code moved.
  # What is actually true instead: a PR exists, or it does not; CI has an
  # opinion on its exact commit, or it does not yet; and GitHub eventually
  # says merged or closed. That is `opened → landed → (needs_fix |
  # merged | closed)`, and nothing about it needed a fourth word — see
  # `Land`'s own comment for why `landed` covers both "just opened" and
  # "fixed and re-pushed" without a fifth state to tell them apart.
  #
  # BORN OPENED, THE SAME WAY `Patch` IS BORN OPENED and `Bug` is born
  # PROVEN (that aggregate's own comment) — there is no state for
  # "somebody is thinking about doing this." The ledger's job starts once
  # the PR is real, not before.
  #
  # `Open` AND `Land` ARE TWO COMMANDS, DELIBERATELY, where `Patch.Open`
  # gets the commit in one shot. The split exists for exactly one reason:
  # `ImprovementCiWatch` below has to `starts_on` ONE event, and that
  # event has to be the SAME one a human or agent dispatches again after
  # a `Regress` — the identical trick `Bug::Fix` already relies on
  # (reused for the first fix and every re-fix after `Bug.Regress`; see
  # that command's own comment). Folding `commit` into `Open` would mean
  # a re-landed commit has nowhere to go but a second `Open` — refused
  # outright as `AlreadyExists`, since `Open` is this aggregate's own
  # creating command and a number only gets created once.
  aggregate "Improvement" do
    description "One pull request landed for deliberate, non-bugfix work — a domain-modeling addition, a tool, an aggregate this very practice needed — number, branch, and the exact commit that makes watching its CI a lookup instead of a guess, the same discipline `Patch` already keeps for a bug's own fix."

    # OPTIONAL, AND SET ONLY AT `Open` — the same shape `hop_chain.
    # bluebook`'s own `Proposal` already establishes for a reference that
    # is real some of the time (`reference_to Engagement, optional:
    # true`, restated on the creating command `Draft` for the identical
    # reason: "a nil/never-set reference is real state a hop has to
    # answer for, and the command input and retained relationship
    # therefore state the same optionality explicitly"). Most of this
    # session's real infra PRs began life as a cited `Angle` a past sweep
    # left in the backlog, but not all deliberate work does — a
    # spontaneous fix-the-tooling PR with no prior lead is exactly as
    # real, and forcing a citation onto it would be inventing an Angle
    # after the fact to satisfy a required field, the identical dishonesty
    # `Expectation`'s own comment already refuses for a check.
    reference_to Angle, optional: true

    identified_by :number

    attribute :number, ImprovementNumber
    attribute :url,    ImprovementUrl
    attribute :branch, ImprovementBranch
    attribute :commit, CommitRef
    attribute :title,  ImprovementTitle
    # WHEN — see `Patch.opened_at`'s own comment; the same field, for the
    # same cap, restated per aggregate the way this file already does.
    attribute :opened_at, Instant

    value_object "Instant" do
      attribute :value, Integer, default: 0
      invariant("an instant is not before the epoch") { !value.negative? }
    end

    value_object "ImprovementNumber" do
      attribute :value, Integer
      invariant("an improvement is numbered") { value.positive? }
    end

    value_object "ImprovementUrl" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("an improvement says where it lives") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "ImprovementBranch" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("an improvement says which branch it is on") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # REDECLARED, NOT SHARED — this file's own established habit
    # (`Engineer`, `Instant`, `CommitRef` are each redeclared per
    # aggregate; `Patch`'s own `CommitRef` comment says why once for all
    # of them). ABSENT UNTIL `Land`, ON PURPOSE — the same reason `Bug`
    # leaves `commit` unset at `Log` and fills it only at `Fix`: this
    # attribute exists to be watched, and there is nothing to watch until
    # a commit is actually on the record.
    value_object "CommitRef" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '^[0-9a-fA-F]{7,40}$'
    end

    value_object "ImprovementTitle" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("an improvement is titled") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    value_object "AngleRef" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # BOTH ENDINGS ALSO REACHABLE STRAIGHT FROM "opened" — the `Merge`/
    # `Close` commands below say so already ("the same `opened →
    # merged`/`closed` shape `Patch` already keeps"), and GitHub itself
    # does not wait for a `Land` before merging or closing a PR: a
    # deliberate proof-only PR (never meant to merge, closed the moment
    # its point was made — real example, PR #543) can be closed by a
    # human while this ledger still has it at "opened", having never been
    # `Land`ed at all. Confirmed live, not assumed: `bin/qa_pr_check`
    # crashed with an uncaught `LifecycleRefused` the first time
    # `retire_if_settled?` tried exactly this — "opened" missing here was
    # the whole gap, matched by `Patch`'s own `Close`/`Merge`, both
    # `from: "opened"`, with no "landed" state to have skipped.
    lifecycle :status, default: "opened" do
      transition "Land"    => "landed",   from: ["opened", "needs_fix"]
      transition "Regress" => "needs_fix", from: "landed"
      transition "Merge"   => "merged",   from: ["opened", "landed", "needs_fix"]
      transition "Close"   => "closed",   from: ["opened", "landed", "needs_fix"]
    end

    # DISPATCHED BY THE ORCHESTRATOR, THE SAME MOMENT AND FOR THE SAME
    # REASON `Patch.Open`'s own comment already gives: whoever opens the
    # PR already has its number/url/branch/title from `gh pr create`'s
    # own return value, and this is where that gets written down instead
    # of re-derived later. See `.claude/skills/hecks_qa/SKILL.md`'s own
    # bullet on when this fires instead of `patch.open`.
    command "Open" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Record that a deliberate, non-bugfix piece of work has been opened as a pull request, before its own commit is known to be worth watching"

      reference_to Angle, optional: true
      attribute :number, ImprovementNumber
      attribute :url,    ImprovementUrl
      attribute :branch, ImprovementBranch
      attribute :title,  ImprovementTitle

      emits "ImprovementOpened"
    end

    # THE EVENT `ImprovementCiWatch` STARTS ON, AND THE ONE A RE-LANDING
    # FIRES AGAIN — see this aggregate's own header comment on why `Open`
    # and `Land` are two commands rather than one.
    #
    # `number` IS RESTATED, THE SAME REDUNDANT-ON-ITS-FACE, LOAD-BEARING
    # REASON `Bug::Fix`'s own comment gives in full for `reference`: an
    # emitted event carries exactly the arguments it was dispatched with,
    # never the record's own identity riding along for free, so an
    # absent `number` here would be an absent `number` for the rest of
    # `ImprovementCiWatch`'s own saga memory — silently, the one failure
    # mode this whole chapter exists to hunt.
    command "Land" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Record the commit this PR is now at, so CI can be watched for it — whether that is the commit it was opened with, or a fresh one pushed after a NeedsFix"

      reference_to Improvement
      attribute :number, ImprovementNumber
      attribute :commit, CommitRef

      sets :commit

      emits "ImprovementLanded"
    end

    # `Regress` — THE SAME WORD `Bug` ALREADY USES for the identical
    # idea, deliberately shared rather than a second verb for one
    # meaning: "put it back in front of somebody when what looked done
    # stops holding." `ImprovementCiWatch` below is the only dispatcher —
    # see that process manager's own header for why there is no
    # "investigating" for this to regress back TO.
    command "Regress" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Put landed work back in front of somebody when its own commit stops holding"

      reference_to Improvement
      emits "ImprovementRegressed"
    end

    # OPENED THEN ONE OF TWO ENDINGS GITHUB ITSELF REPORTS — the same
    # `opened → merged`/`closed` shape `Patch` already keeps, restated
    # here rather than shared for the identical reason `Patch`'s own
    # lifecycle comment gives: both endings are terminal, and reachable
    # from `needs_fix` too, because GitHub can merge or close a PR
    # regardless of what this ledger's own CI watch last heard.
    command "Merge" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Record that GitHub merged it"

      reference_to Improvement
      emits "ImprovementMerged"
    end

    command "Close" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Record that GitHub closed it without merging"

      reference_to Improvement
      emits "ImprovementClosed"
    end

    query "Open" do
      description "Every PR we've landed for deliberate, non-bugfix work that's still open, by number — bin/qa_pr_check's own second worklist, the same shape Patch.Open already gives for a bug's own fix. For each of these, ask gh for exactly this number's CI status; nothing here is a guess."
      where(status: { in: "opened,landed,needs_fix" })
      order_by :number
    end

    query "NeedsFix" do
      description "Landed work whose own commit came back red — what ImprovementCiWatch put back in front of somebody, and the list a human or agent clears with a fresh Land."
      where(status: "needs_fix")
      order_by :number
    end

    query "ForAngle" do
      description "Every improvement ever landed against one angle — the duplicate check, the same shape Patch.ForBug already gives for a bug."
      attribute :angle_id, AngleRef
      where(angle: :angle_id)
      order_by :number
    end

    query "All" do
      description "Every improvement ever landed — what a runner reads back to reconcile against GitHub's own list."
      order_by :number
    end
  end

  # ── is it safe to ship ───────────────────────────────────────────────

  # A GREEN CI RUN, AGAINST THE EXACT COMMIT.
  #
  # "Safe to deploy" is not a question about now, it is a record about a
  # commit: CI passed at two o'clock, you have pushed twice since, and the
  # green belongs to what it ran against. So a clearance is minted per commit
  # and nothing has to make it stale — a later commit simply has none of its
  # own, which is the same thing said without needing a clock.
  aggregate "Clearance" do
    description "One CI run against one commit, and therefore the only answer to whether that commit is safe to ship."

    identified_by :commit

    attribute :commit,  CommitRef
    attribute :summary, RunSummary

    value_object "CommitRef" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '^[0-9a-fA-F]{7,40}$'
    end

    value_object "RunSummary" do
      attribute :value, String, pattern: '[^ \t\n\r]'
      invariant("a run says what it ran and what came back") { !value.to_s.empty? }
    end

    lifecycle :status, default: "running" do
      transition "Passed" => "green", from: "running"
      transition "Failed" => "red",   from: "running"
    end

    command "Start" do
      role "QA engineer"
      goal "Put a commit through CI"

      attribute :commit, CommitRef

      emits "ClearanceStarted"
    end

    command "Passed" do
      role "System"
      goal "Record that the suite went green against this commit"

      reference_to Clearance
      attribute :summary, RunSummary

      sets :summary

      emits "ClearanceGiven"
    end

    command "Failed" do
      role "System"
      goal "Record that the suite went red against this commit"

      # `refusal`, NOT `summary`, and the rename is load-bearing. A policy
      # re-enters its target with the answering event payload VERBATIM, and
      # the runtime names what came back from a refused ask `refusal` — so a
      # command expecting `summary` here would be handed a key it never
      # declared and refuse the very failure it exists to record. The word
      # is the framework's; `sets` is where it becomes ours.
      reference_to Clearance
      attribute :refusal, RunSummary

      sets :summary, to: :refusal

      emits "ClearanceRefused"
    end

    # THE DEPLOY QUESTION, ASKED THE ONLY WAY IT CAN BE ANSWERED — name the
    # commit you are about to ship. An empty answer is a no, and it is a no
    # for the right reason: not "CI is red" but "nothing has vouched for
    # THIS".
    query "For" do
      description "Is this exact commit cleared? Ask with the sha you are about to deploy; an empty answer is a no, including when nobody has run CI on it at all."
      attribute :commit, CommitRef
      where(commit: :commit)
      where(status: "green")
      order_by :commit
    end

    query "Red" do
      description "Commits CI refused, with what it said."
      where(status: "red")
      order_by :commit
    end

    query "All" do
      description "Every CI run ever recorded, one per commit."
      order_by :commit
    end
  end

  # ── noticing when a fix stops holding ───────────────────────────────

  # A HUMAN USED TO BE THE ONLY THING WATCHING FOR THIS. `Bug.Fix` records
  # a commit; CI runs against it, eventually, somewhere else; and until
  # this procedure existed, nothing in the ledger ever put those two facts
  # back together on its own. A fix that looked right locally and broke on
  # CI's own fresh build sat `fixed` — or worse, `verified` — indefinitely,
  # and the only way anyone found out was a person noticing a PR had gone
  # red and saying so out loud. That is exactly the shape this whole
  # chapter exists to hunt: the ledger PROMISED a fix, the world quietly
  # disagreed, and nobody but a human happened to be looking.
  #
  # `Regress` ALREADY SAID THE RIGHT WORD — "put a bug back when its fix
  # stopped holding" — and nothing had ever dispatched it. This procedure
  # is the thing that finally does, every time, driven by the one fact
  # that actually settles the question: what `Clearance` says about the
  # exact commit `Fix` recorded.
  #
  # CORRELATES BY THE COMMIT, NOT THE BUG'S OWN REFERENCE — commit is the
  # one scalar both sides of this conversation can name. `Bug.Fix` sets it
  # directly, so `BugFixed` carries it in payload; `Clearance` is
  # IDENTIFIED by it, so `ClearanceGiven`/`ClearanceRefused` correlate with
  # no field of their own to dig at all — `SagaInterpreter::Correlation`'s
  # own third tier reads a mutating aggregate's `event.id` exactly when the
  # correlated field IS that aggregate's own identity, and `commit` is
  # `Clearance`'s. The bug's own reference travels a different way: it
  # rides along in `BugFixed`'s payload too (see `Bug.Fix`'s own comment on
  # why `reference` is declared there, restated), captured once into this
  # saga's own memory at the start and read back out the one time it is
  # needed — addressing the SAME bug when its commit comes back red.
  #
  # `ClearanceGiven` ENDS IT. CI passed; there is nothing left to watch
  # for; `ends_on` deletes the instance the moment that event arrives for
  # this commit. `ClearanceRefused` does the one thing this procedure
  # exists to do and stops there — no `ends_on` names it, the same way
  # `ExternalSettlement`'s own `:refused` branch leaves `"returned"`
  # un-ended (examples/banking's own precedent, not an oversight here): the
  # regression is on the record the moment `Bug.Regress` lands, and an
  # instance sitting in `"regressed"` forever costs nothing — its commit
  # already has its answer.
  process_manager "BugCiWatch" do
    correlates_by :"commit.value"
    starts_on Bug::BugFixed
    ends_on   Clearance::ClearanceGiven

    # `bug:` — Bug's OWN reference key (`Naming.reference_key("Bug")`),
    # the same alias Settlement's own `transfer:`/`wire:` legs already
    # address a record by, never the bare `reference:` field name. The
    # VALUE stays `:reference` — the one field `BugFixed` was made to
    # carry, sitting in this saga's own memory since the moment it began.
    transition Clearance::ClearanceRefused => "regressed", from: "watching" do
      dispatch Bug::Regress, with: { bug: :reference }
    end
  end

  # ── noticing when landed work stops holding ─────────────────────────

  # THE SAME WATCH `BugCiWatch` ALREADY KEEPS, above, aimed at deliberate
  # work instead of a proven-wrong finding — `Improvement.Land` records a
  # commit; CI runs against it, eventually, somewhere else; this is what
  # puts those two facts back together on its own, the identical shape
  # `BugCiWatch`'s own header already explains end to end (why the
  # correlation is by commit and not by this record's own reference, why
  # `ClearanceGiven` simply ends the instance and `ClearanceRefused` is
  # the only transition, why an instance left sitting in "regressed"
  # forever costs nothing). Read that comment; nothing about the
  # mechanism differs here.
  #
  # WHAT DOES DIFFER: THERE IS NO "investigating" TO PUT THIS BACK INTO.
  # `Bug::Regress` returns a bug to a state where a person re-examines
  # WHY the code is wrong, because that question is real for a bug and
  # was already answered once. Nothing was ever wrong here — deliberate
  # work went red on CI for the ordinary reasons deliberate work does
  # (a flake, a merge with `main`, a test the new code itself needed to
  # update) — so `Improvement::Regress` lands the record in `needs_fix`
  # instead: not "go investigate," just "this needs attention before it
  # can be trusted," which is exactly what a human or agent notices and
  # clears the same way `Bug.Regress`'s own comment describes — a fresh
  # `Improvement.Land`, carrying whatever new commit addresses it, which
  # is the SAME event this saga started on and so starts a fresh
  # instance watching it, the identical mechanism `BugCiWatch`'s own
  # reuse of `Bug::Fix` already relies on.
  process_manager "ImprovementCiWatch" do
    correlates_by :"commit.value"
    starts_on Improvement::ImprovementLanded
    ends_on   Clearance::ClearanceGiven

    # `improvement:` — Improvement's OWN reference key
    # (`Naming.reference_key("Improvement")`), the same alias `bug:`
    # already is for `Bug::Regress` above. The VALUE is `:number`, the
    # one field `ImprovementLanded` was made to carry (see `Land`'s own
    # comment on why it is restated), sitting in this saga's own memory
    # since the moment it began.
    transition Clearance::ClearanceRefused => "regressed", from: "watching" do
      dispatch Improvement::Regress, with: { improvement: :number }
    end
  end

  # ── the composed reads ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  #
  # THE ONE THING A QUERY CANNOT DO IS COUNT. Every question above answers
  # with rows and leaves the arithmetic to whoever is reading — which is fine
  # for `queue` (you work the first one) and useless for "how are we doing",
  # where the whole answer IS the shape of the pile. A rootless report with
  # `group_by` nests an aggregate own whole table by its own field, so the
  # count is the size of each group and nobody has to hold twenty numbers in
  # their head.
  #
  # ROOTLESS ON PURPOSE. These centre on no record: there is no one bug whose
  # view this is. That is exactly the shape `group_by` exists for, and why
  # these take no id at dispatch.

  # The number worth watching is not the total. It is the share of it that
  # turned out not to be a bug — `withdrawn` beside `verified` says whether
  # this practice is finding real things or crying wolf, and no single query
  # can put those two side by side.
  read_model "BugsByStatus" do
    description "Every bug sorted into what became of it — the tally. The share that is withdrawn, beside the share verified, is the honesty check on the whole practice."
    include Bug
    group_by :status, :reference
  end

  # WHO IS FINDING THINGS, which matters once more than one agent is
  # sweeping: a submitter with nothing but withdrawn reports is a different
  # problem from a quiet one.
  read_model "BugsBySubmitter" do
    description "Every bug sorted by who reported it, then by its own reference — what each agent has actually found."
    include Bug
    group_by :submitter, :reference
  end

  # THE FILING PILE AT A GLANCE. `submitting` rows are calls whose outcome
  # nobody recorded and `abandoned` rows are ones nothing automatic will
  # touch again — both are only alarming as a COUNT, which is the reading
  # the per-status queries cannot give.
  read_model "TicketsByStatus" do
    description "Every ticket sorted by where it got to — stuck filings and abandoned ones are only visible as a count."
    include Ticket
    group_by :status, :reference
  end

  # THE SAME TALLY `BugsByStatus` GIVES, for the sibling ledger — how much
  # deliberate work is sitting in `needs_fix` is only alarming as a COUNT,
  # the identical reading `TicketsByStatus`'s own comment gives for a
  # stuck filing.
  read_model "ImprovementsByStatus" do
    description "Every improvement sorted into what became of it — landed, needing a fix, merged, or closed, at a glance."
    include Improvement
    group_by :status, :number
  end

  # ── what happens without anybody asking ──────────────────────────────

  # THE FILING, DRIVEN. `Raise` records the intent; this makes the call.
  # Splitting them is what lets a crash between the two leave a row in
  # `Ticket.Raised` rather than a lie, and it is why the ask is a reaction to
  # an event rather than something a command does on the side.
  # THE SUITE ANSWERS THE CLEARANCE. `Start` puts a commit up; the ask runs
  # the suite; whichever ending comes back records itself. Nothing here reads
  # a result and decides — the adapter already decided, by answering or
  # refusing, which is the only branch a policy can express.
  # THE LOOP CLOSED FROM EITHER END. `Ticket.Close` is the only way a ticket
  # leaves `filed`, and nothing in this repository can know it happened —
  # somebody fixes the bug on GitHub and clicks close. So the fact has to
  # arrive from outside, by whichever route reaches us first, and both routes
  # already say the same word.
  policy "CloseWhenClosedUpstream" do
    on      "Ticket.IssueClosedUpstream"
    trigger Ticket::Close
  end

  policy "ClearOnPass" do
    on      "Clearance.SuitePassed"
    trigger Clearance::Passed
  end

  policy "RefuseOnFail" do
    on      "Clearance.SuiteFailed"
    trigger Clearance::Failed
  end

  policy "FileWhenSubmitted" do
    on      "Ticket.TicketSubmitted"
    trigger Ticket::IssueTracker::File
  end

  policy "RecordTheIssue" do
    on      "Ticket.IssueFiled"
    trigger Ticket::Filed
  end

  policy "RecordTheRefusal" do
    on      "Ticket.IssueFilingRefused"
    trigger Ticket::Refused
  end

  # THREE TRIES. The cap is not here — a policy holds no state and would ask
  # forever. It is the `given` on `Retry`, reading the `attempts` the refusal
  # incremented, which is banking's ScheduledPayment shape exactly. Past the
  # cap the retry refuses, the reaction records that it refused, and the
  # ticket sits in `Refused` for a person — which is what the report reads.
  policy "AskAgain" do
    on      "Ticket.TicketFilingRefused"
    trigger Ticket::Retry
  end

  policy "AskOnceMore" do
    on      "Ticket.TicketRetried"
    trigger Ticket::IssueTracker::File
  end

  # `Raised` MEANS COMPOSED, NOT SENT. Nothing reacts to `TicketRaised` on
  # purpose — a ticket sits there until somebody decides it goes, which is
  # what makes `Ticket.Raised` a list worth reading rather than a millisecond
  # of internal state.

  # NO POLICY FOR THE ROTATION, and it was written out and taken back. "On
  # SweepConcluded, release the target" is one dispatch and looks right — but
  # a policy carries the EVENT's payload, and `Target.Release` needs the
  # target's own id and the sweep that swept it, which that payload does not
  # have. A reaction that cannot carry what its target needs refuses at run
  # time and reads, in the log, as though it had worked. The runner releases
  # the target, and `Target.Held` is where a stale claim stays visible until
  # somebody does.
end
